      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the constants \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in the function \\(f(x) = ax + b\\), we start by using the given equation \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Given that \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we can substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5\n\\]\n\nSimplified, this gives us:\n\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\), so we can equate the expressions we obtained:\n\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we match the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides of the equation:\n\n1. For the \\(x\\) terms: \\(2a = 3\\)\n2. For the constant terms: \\(2b - 5 = 4\\)\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, solving the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find \\(a + b\\):\n\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
