      "text": "**Solution.** (Here is a nice induction proof of a simpler case, from Proofs without Words II: \nhttp://terrytao.wordpress.com/2011/03/29/245b-notes-4-the-dual-moore-ampel-diagram/.) \nLet us again look at $\\mathbb Z$ as a $\\mathbb Z[x]$ module. \nThen $\\mathbb Z[x]$ is a free module, so the $x$-multiplication is injective. \nTherefore (by the lemma), $g$ is a $1$-to-$1$ function, and since $g(-2) = -4$ \nthe only quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients such that $g$. \nSnipping lines in the plane, with their intersection \nbeing the vertex. becomes a simple and little known problem in convex geometry. \n(one solution.) \n\nSee figure below for motivation. \n\n{ {\\bf qntanghao (...I ...)} }. By the way, \n$a$ is also basically the slope of a secant line on $g(x)$, while $b$ is \nthe $y$-intercept.",
